   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The General Confederation of Lebanese Workers (CGTL) and the National Federation of Worker and Employee Trade Unions in Lebanon (FENASOL) conducted a gender audit with a view to develop their organizations&#x2019; structures and bylaws to support organizing women workers, and to enable women workers bigger roles in the Union management and decision-making positions.
 
Based on the gender audit report, the FENASOL adopted a workplan to organize women workers in specific sectors and started amending its bylaws and regulations to be gender sensitive and create better enabling environment for women workers to take leadership positions. FENASOL has been supported to outreach 114 domestic workers (76 migrants among them). 20 domestic workers migrants and nationals, were capacitated to prepare them for leading positions at the FENASOL Domestic Workers Union (DWU). The trainings included information about legal rights in the Lebanese labour law, ILO conventions nos. 87 and 98 on freedom of association and collective bargaining, and Convention no. 189 on domestic work. 

The General Confederation of Lebanese Workers (CGTL) and the National Federation of Worker and Employee Trade Unions in Lebanon (FENASOL) formed a joint committee and adopted an action plan to formalize workers in the informal economy in two economic sectors: agri-food and trade, mainly targeting workers in the pastry and confectionary industry, dairy production, and commerce. Through funding from RBSA, the ILO provided technical and financial support to build capacities of both the General Confederation of Lebanese Workers (CGTL) and the National Federation of Worker and Employee Trade Unions in Lebanon (FENASOL) to cooperate in organizing workers in the informal economy, especially women and youth workers, and to develop an enabling environment inside their organizations for women workers to take leadership positions. 

In addition, technical and financial support was provided to FENASOL to enhance its legal services to protect and organize migrant domestic workers. FENASOL has been supported to establish a legal unit to provide legal assistance for DWU in Beirut and the regions coordinated by an lawyer to provide legal support including identifying possible collective cases and areas for FENASOL&#x2019;s strategic advocacy, as well as discussing referral pathways with different embassies and civil society, assisted by a coordinator to look over domestic workers labour complaints/grievances.</narrative>
   </description>
